{"ex-104-1":{"number":"1","title":"Heating curve of water","question":"<p>Some water is heated at a constant power. The variation of the temperature of the water with time is as shown.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/813e0aa66e89f15dc517dd23.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:351.2px\"/></p><p>(a) Briefly explain why the graph is flat at stage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Does the water absorb energy at stage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>?</p><p>(c) Suggest a value for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) The water is boiling at stage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Therefore, its temperature remains constant.</p><p>(b) Yes</p><p>(c) 100 °C</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>T is the temperature when the water boils.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 1  |  Chapter 104  |  Source PDF p. 5","source":{"name":"104 Examples.docx","sha256":"cf61ee6b3e6c1f0f9a6c3df7c07d8825802d5532bd00e92ed86f9dc08cef9974","block":2},"id":"ex-104-1","chapter":"104"},"ex-104-2":{"number":"2","title":"Energy needed to melt ice","question":"<p>Find the energy needed to melt 0.25 kg of ice at 0 °C. Given that the specific latent heat of fusion of ice is 3.34 × 10<sup>5</sup> J kg<sup>−1</sup>.</p>","answer":"<p>Energy required = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.25 × (3.34 × 10<sup>5</sup>) = 8.35 × 10<sup>4</sup> J</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 1  |  Chapter 104  |  Source PDF p. 9","source":{"name":"104 Examples.docx","sha256":"cf61ee6b3e6c1f0f9a6c3df7c07d8825802d5532bd00e92ed86f9dc08cef9974","block":16},"id":"ex-104-2","chapter":"104"},"ex-104-3":{"number":"3","title":"Steam and hot water","question":"<p>Find the energy released when</p><p>(a) water of mass 15 g at 100 °C is cooled to 50 °C;</p><p>(b) steam of mass 15 g at 100 °C is cooled to water at 50 °C.</p><p>Given that the specific heat capacity of water is 4200 J kg<sup>−1</sup> °C<sup>−1</sup> and the specific latent heat of vaporization of water is 2.26 × 10<sup>6</sup> J kg<sup>−1</sup>.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Energy released = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.015 × 4200 × (100 − 50) = 3150 J</p><p>(b) Energy released = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>= 0.015 × (2.26 × 10<sup>6</sup>) + 3150</p><p>= 37 050 J</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Simple diagram:</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/d617234f56aeb2d57eb5d25b.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:174.8px\"/></p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 1  |  Chapter 104  |  Source PDF p. 9","source":{"name":"104 Examples.docx","sha256":"cf61ee6b3e6c1f0f9a6c3df7c07d8825802d5532bd00e92ed86f9dc08cef9974","block":21},"id":"ex-104-3","chapter":"104"},"ex-104-4":{"number":"4","title":"Finding the specific latent heat of fusion of ice","question":"<p>Jenny carries out Experiment 4c and obtains the following results:</p><p>Mass of water collected in experiment beaker <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.050 kg</p><p>Mass of water collected in control beaker <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.014 kg</p><p>Energy supplied by heater = 14 200 J</p><p>(a) Write the equation used to calculate the specific latent heat of fusion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of ice in the experiment.</p><p>(b) Calculate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> using the above data.</p><p>(c) The standard value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is 3.34 × 10<sup>5</sup> J kg<sup>−1</sup>. Find the percentage error of the result in (b).</p><p>(d) State a possible source of error.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>(b) Mass of ice melted by heater = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.050 − 0.014 = 0.036 kg</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 3.94 × 10<sup>5</sup> J kg<sup>−1</sup></p><p>(c) Percentage error = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 100% = 18.0%</p><p>(d) Not all the energy supplied by the heater is absorbed by the ice.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 1  |  Chapter 104  |  Source PDF p. 12","source":{"name":"104 Examples.docx","sha256":"cf61ee6b3e6c1f0f9a6c3df7c07d8825802d5532bd00e92ed86f9dc08cef9974","block":36},"id":"ex-104-4","chapter":"104"},"ex-104-5":{"number":"5","title":"Finding the specific latent heat of vaporization of water","question":"<p>Jimmy carries out Experiment 4d using an old type of kilowatt-hour meter. With this type of kilowatt-hour meter, the energy supplied through it can be calculated from the number of rotations of the disc on it.</p><p>The calibration of the kilowatt-hour meter is 600 rotations/kW h, meaning that the energy supplied per revolution of the disc is 6000 J.</p><p>The following results are obtained in the experiment.</p><p>Initial mass of water <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.82 kg</p><p>Final mass of water <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.72 kg</p><p>Number of rotations counted = 41</p><p>(a) Write the equation used to calculate the specific latent heat of vaporization of water <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> in the experiment.</p><p>(b) Calculate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> using the above data.</p><p>(c) Jimmy suggests adding a lid to reduce experimental error. Explain why this is not a good suggestion.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>(b) Mass of water vaporized = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.82 − 0.72 = 0.10 kg</p><p>Energy supplied to vaporize the water = 6000 × 41 = 246 000 J</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2.46 × 10<sup>6</sup> J kg<sup>−1</sup></p><p>(c) Water vapour would condense back into water on the inner surface of the lid and drip back into the beaker.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 1  |  Chapter 104  |  Source PDF p. 14, 15","source":{"name":"104 Examples.docx","sha256":"cf61ee6b3e6c1f0f9a6c3df7c07d8825802d5532bd00e92ed86f9dc08cef9974","block":52},"id":"ex-104-5","chapter":"104"},"ex-104-6":{"number":"6","title":"Reheating the soup","question":"<p>Mary reheats 250 g of tomato soup at 4 °C using a steamer. In this process, 20 g of steam at 100 °C condenses on the tomato soup. The specific heat capacities of the tomato soup and water are 3700 J kg<sup>−1</sup> °C<sup>−1</sup> and 4200 J kg<sup>−1</sup> °C<sup>−1</sup> respectively. The specific latent heat of vaporization of water is 2.26 × 10<sup>6</sup> J kg<sup>−1</sup>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/78d73f72b2a72e51e9b97d9b.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:228.3px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the final temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the tomato soup. Assume there is no energy loss to the surroundings.</p><p>(b) Suppose the energy loss to the surroundings is not negligible. Compare the actual final temperature with the result in (a).</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Energy lost by steam = energy gained by soup</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>w</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>w</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>w</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>0.02 × (2.26 × 10<sup>6</sup>) + 0.02 × 4200 × (100 − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>) = 0.25 × 3700 × (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> − 4)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 56.8 °C</p><p>(b) As some energy is lost to the surroundings, less energy would be absorbed by the tomato soup. Therefore, the final temperature would be lower.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 1  |  Chapter 104  |  Source PDF p. 17","source":{"name":"104 Examples.docx","sha256":"cf61ee6b3e6c1f0f9a6c3df7c07d8825802d5532bd00e92ed86f9dc08cef9974","block":69},"id":"ex-104-6","chapter":"104"},"ex-104-7":{"number":"7","title":"Reheating vegetables","question":"<p>Mandy uses a steamer to reheat 800 g of vegetables at 25 °C.</p><p>Given:</p><p>specific latent heat of vaporization of water = 2.26 × 10<sup>6</sup> J kg<sup>−1</sup></p><p>specific heat capacity of water = 4200 J kg<sup>−1</sup> °C<sup>−1</sup></p><p>specific heat capacity of the vegetables = 3000 J kg<sup>−1</sup> °C<sup>−1</sup></p><p>When the water boils, Mandy puts the vegetables into the steamer for 10 minutes. Suppose half of the steam condenses on the vegetables, and energy is supplied to the water at 500 W.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/6572fcc6d5841c4cd0e3ff07.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:346.2px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the total mass of steam condensing on the vegetables.</p><p>(b) Estimate the final temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the vegetables.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Energy supplied by steamer = energy gained by 100 °C water</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>500 × (10 × 60) = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> × (2.26 × 10<sup>6</sup>)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.1062 kg</p><p>Total mass of steam condensing on the vegetables = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 0.1062</p><p>= 0.0531 kg</p><p>(b) Energy lost by steam = energy gained by</p><p>vegetables</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>w</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>w</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>w</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>0.0531 × (2.26 × 10<sup>6</sup>) + 0.0531 × 4200 × (100 − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>) = 0.8 × 3000 × (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> − 25)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 77.1 °C</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Water of mass m at 100 °C becomes steam at 100 °C in 10 minutes.</p><p>Editorial check</p><p>Editorial check: The printed solution is retained above. With the stated 500 W, the mass of steam produced is 0.1327 kg, of which 0.0664 kg condenses. Substitution in the energy balance gives a final temperature of 88.8 °C. The printed 0.1062 kg and 77.1 °C are inconsistent with the stated power.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 1  |  Chapter 104  |  Source PDF p. 20","source":{"name":"104 Examples.docx","sha256":"cf61ee6b3e6c1f0f9a6c3df7c07d8825802d5532bd00e92ed86f9dc08cef9974","block":81},"id":"ex-104-7","chapter":"104"},"ex-104-8":{"number":"8","title":"Heating water","question":"<p>500 g of water is heated at a constant power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and a heating curve (orange line) is obtained as shown below.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/d82ffd137af37c2b0f8fdb78.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:309.0px\"/></p><p>Take the specific heat capacity of water as 4200 J kg<sup>−1</sup> °C<sup>−1</sup>.</p><p>(a) Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Hence, estimate the specific latent heat of vaporization of water <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) Another liquid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of mass 500 g is heated at the same power. The heating curve (green line) is also shown in the figure. Compare the following quantities of liquid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> with those of water and explain briefly.</p><p>(i) Boiling point</p><p>(ii) Specific heat capacity</p><p>(iii) Specific latent heat of vaporization</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Energy supplied in heating = energy gained by water</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> × (2 × 60) = 0.5 × 4200 × (100 − 20)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1400 W</p><p>(b) Energy supplied in heating = energy gained by water</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>1400 × (15 × 60) = 0.5<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 2.52 × 10<sup>6</sup> J kg<sup>−1</sup></p><p>(c) (i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> boils at a lower temperature. Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> has a lower boiling point.</p><p>(ii) For the same mass heated at the same power, the temperature of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> rises at a lower rate. Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> has a higher specific heat capacity.</p><p>(iii) For the same mass heated at the same power, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> takes less time to vaporize. Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> has a lower specific latent heat of vaporization.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The temperature of water rises from 20 °C to 100 °C during 0−2 min.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>All the water becomes steam during 2−17 min.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>A higher specific heat capacity means more energy is needed to raise the temperature by</p><p>1 °C.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>A higher specific latent heat means more energy is needed to change 1 kg of a substance from one state to another.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 1  |  Chapter 104  |  Source PDF p. 21","source":{"name":"104 Examples.docx","sha256":"cf61ee6b3e6c1f0f9a6c3df7c07d8825802d5532bd00e92ed86f9dc08cef9974","block":109},"id":"ex-104-8","chapter":"104"},"ex-104-9":{"number":"9","title":"Cooling effect of sweating","question":"<p>Yvonne of mass 50 kg is exercising and is sweating heavily.</p><p>(a) How much energy is taken away by 650 g of sweat when it evaporates? Assume the specific latent heat of vaporization of sweat is 2.26 × 10<sup>6</sup> J kg<sup>−1</sup>.</p><p>(b) If this amount of energy was not removed from the body, by how much would Yvonne’s body temperature increase? Take the average specific heat capacity of the human body to be 3500 J kg<sup>−1</sup> °C<sup>−1</sup>.</p><p>(c) Explain why evaporation occurs in terms of molecular motion.</p><p>(d) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>H</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>o</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>k</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> occurs when our body temperature becomes too high. Explain why the risk of getting heat stroke on a humid day is higher.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Energy taken away = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>l</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>= 0.65 × (2.26 × 10<sup>6</sup>)</p><p>= 1.469 × 10<sup>6</sup> ≈ 1.47 × 10<sup>6</sup> J</p><p>(b) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p>1.469 × 10<sup>6</sup> = 50 × 3500 × Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 8.39 °C</p><p>Yvonne’s body temperature would increase by 8.39 °C.</p><p>(c) Some molecules at the surface of a liquid gain enough KE. They escape into the space above the liquid and become vapour molecules.</p><p>(d) Sweat evaporates slowly on a humid day. As such, energy cannot be removed from a human body through the evaporation of sweat effectively. Therefore, the risk of getting heat stroke is higher.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 1  |  Chapter 104  |  Source PDF p. 31, 32","source":{"name":"104 Examples.docx","sha256":"cf61ee6b3e6c1f0f9a6c3df7c07d8825802d5532bd00e92ed86f9dc08cef9974","block":142},"id":"ex-104-9","chapter":"104"}}